STATUS: Generate one standalone, reusable Orc fortress marker for Urkaaaz's
campaign maps. Base its design on the newest attached campaign-map screenshot.
This is a separate transparent overlay asset, not a map, screenshot, or
gameplay fortress sprite.

## Reference input

- Attach the newest campaign-map screenshot showing the blue-banner Orc
  fortresses. Use the fortress landmarks at levels 2–9 as the primary visual
  reference; the illuminated level 2 fortress shows the structure most
  clearly. This screenshot supersedes all earlier fortress-art references.
- Match the screenshot's compact, layered stronghold silhouette, dark stone
  and timber construction, small navy banners with ivory skull emblems, and
  warm amber windows and torchlight.
- Treat the screenshot's level plaques, numbers, locks, checkmark, and level 2
  glow as separate UI. Do not copy any of those elements into the fortress.
- Keep the illustration suitable for every campaign biome. Do not copy the
  map, terrain, route, vegetation, water, sky, or other scenery.

## Output

- Generate exactly one PNG:
  `campaign_map_marker_fortress.png`.
- Save it under `assets_src/campaign/map-markers/`.
- Canvas: exactly 512 x 512 pixels, square, RGBA.
- Use genuine transparent alpha. Do not draw a checkerboard or any opaque or
  colored canvas.
- Center the complete fortress illustration and keep it within the central
  82% of the canvas, with clean transparent margins on every side.
- Export one reusable fortress icon, not a sprite sheet, contact sheet, or
  collection of variants.

## Fortress design

- Depict one unmistakably Orcish fortress landmark, viewed from the elevated
  three-quarter angle shown in the campaign screenshot.
- Build a compact, readable silhouette from a squat dark-stone keep, rough
  timber braces, asymmetrical battlements, a reinforced gate, and a few
  layered towers with blunt spikes or tusk-like details.
- Use the screenshot's deep navy cloth banners with simple ivory skull
  emblems. Do not switch them to crimson or use faction-color cues other than
  those in the reference.
- Add a small number of skull or bone details to the fortress itself, but
  keep the skull banners secondary to the building silhouette.
- Include restrained amber light in windows or attached torches to preserve
  the screenshot's warm, firelit look. Keep all light and shadow tight to the
  building.
- Use weathered charcoal stone, near-black timber, iron straps, navy cloth,
  ivory accents, and limited torch amber. Keep the finish painterly and
  high-contrast, not polished or human-medieval.
- Match the screenshot's level of architectural detail while ensuring the
  keep, gate, and banners remain readable at the campaign marker's 72 dp
  display size.
- Keep the object centered and upright. Preserve the same visual identity
  when it is drawn over both bright and dark map terrain.

## Separation from UI and other assets

- Generate only the fortress illustration. Android draws the level number,
  marker hit target, lock/check status, and selection feedback separately.
- Do not draw any circular or hexagonal badge, marker frame, status ring,
  pedestal, selection glow, lock, checkmark, number plaque, level number,
  label, text, or other UI.
- Do not draw a map, terrain tile, path, biome background, separate scenery,
  surrounding buildings, characters, weapons, or battle effects.
- Do not add a large cast shadow, smoke, flames, or light rays outside the
  fortress silhouette. Keep transparent pixels clean and attached to the
  illustration.
- This is not the large side-view fortress sprite used in gameplay. Use the
  screenshot's campaign-marker camera angle and proportions.
- The boss marker is a separate asset and must not be included.

## Negative prompt

background, opaque background, colored background, checkerboard, map, terrain,
grass tile, path, biome scenery, sky, water, sprite sheet, multiple icons,
multiple fortresses, side-view gameplay fortress, human medieval castle,
clean European stone keep, symmetrical fairytale castle, polished white
stone, red banners, red roof, heraldic shield, fleur-de-lis, sprawling city,
fortress complex, excessive towers, tiny unreadable details, ornate filigree,
characters, soldiers, creatures, weapons, combat, fire effects, explosions,
smoke, text, letters, numbers, labels, logo, watermark, signature, badge,
circular frame, hexagonal frame, marker pin, status ring, selection glow,
level plaque, lock, checkmark, UI, cropped walls, cropped towers, detached
shadow, colored fringe, jagged alpha edges

## Review checklist

- Confirm exactly one centered fortress is present on a 512 x 512 RGBA canvas.
- Verify the background has genuine transparency and clean alpha edges.
- Preview at 72 dp and confirm the keep, gate, and navy skull banners remain
  recognizable.
- Confirm the design matches the blue-banner Orc fortresses in the newest
  screenshot, not the superseded crimson-banner concept.
- Confirm the silhouette contrasts against both light and dark map regions.
- Confirm no badge, plaque, number, status, lock, text, map, or separate
  scenery is baked into the image.
- Confirm the design remains biome-neutral and does not copy the gameplay
  sprite's camera angle or composition.
- Do not import the generated image into Android until it is reviewed and
  approved. Once approved, it can replace the existing campaign fortress
  marker without changing marker anchors or progression logic.
